[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64839,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vaginal adenosis is the presence of benign glandular epithelium (unassociated with endometrioid-type stroma) involving the superficial lamina propria or vaginal epithelium.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64840,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64841,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GA14.Y Other specified acquired abnormalities of vagina</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64842,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64843,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64844,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenosis associated with diethylstilbestrol (DES) exposure is typically in the upper two&nbsp;thirds of the vagina, whereas sporadic forms occur throughout, although there may be overlap {1033667; 30544435}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64845,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenosis occurs over a wide age range. Patients may present with vaginal discharge or pain, or they may be asymptomatic and the adenosis discovered as an incidental finding {30544435; 3699812; 1157359; 29664741}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64846,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenosis is uncommon. Historically, it was more frequent in patients exposed in&nbsp;utero to DES {4829509; 1117962; 22682699}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64847,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In&nbsp;utero DES exposure was a common cause of adenosis before 1970 {6537153; 4829509; 1117962; 5549830}. Adenosis in patients not exposed to DES is most commonly idiopathic but can be congenital, sometimes associated with genitourinary malformations {4829509; 1033667; 29664741; 22317878} or rarely secondary to topical fluorouracil or CO<sub>2</sub> laser treatment {1893363; 20173507}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64848,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vaginal adenosis is speculated to result from a failure of squamous differentiation in M&uuml;llerian duct epithelium due to disruption of p63 (TRP63, transformation-related protein&nbsp;63) {22682699}. Adenosis may also be a response to mucosal trauma {20173507}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64849,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Grossly, adenosis may exhibit a red granular or nodular appearance or may be occult {4829509; 30544435; 3699812}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64850,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Histology shows glands located within the superficial stroma or replacing the squamous epithelium. The glands are tuboendometrioid or mucinous; the mucinous pattern may be endocervical or gastric {6537153; 4829509; 3699812; 29664741}. The tuboendometrioid pattern is characterized by non-mucinous cells, sometimes with cilia; endometrioid-type stroma must be absent. Gastric-type adenosis displays pale/eosinophilic cytoplasm, prominent cell membranes, and sometimes intestinal differentiation with goblet cells {29664741}. Atypical tuboendometrioid and gastric adenosis (adenosis with nuclear atypia) has been reported {6537153; 29664741; 22317878}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64851,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cytology reveals mucinous or non-mucinous columnar cells on vaginal smear {7195652}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64852,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64853,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> benign glandular structures within the superficial vagina; no endometrioid-type stroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64854,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64855,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 340,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenosis can regress secondary to squamous metaplasia {7195652}. Tuboendometrioid and gastric adenosis, respectively, may progress via atypical adenosis to clear cell and gastric-type adenocarcinoma {6537153; 1157359; 29664741; 22317878}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-08T17:05:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]